.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt ACCESS 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm access ,
.Nm faccessat ,
.Nm faccessat2
.Nd check file existence and permissions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn access "const char *path" "int mode"
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn faccessat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "int mode" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn faccessat2 "int dirfd" "const char *path" "int mode" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn access
checks whether
.Fa path
exists and grants the permissions requested by
.Fa mode .
.Dv F_OK
(zero) checks existence only.
The other modes may be combined with bitwise OR:
.Bl -tag -width W_OK
.It Dv R_OK
Read permission.
.It Dv W_OK
Write permission.
.It Dv X_OK
Execute permission for a file, or search permission for a directory.
.El
.Pp
The kernel uses the caller's real user and group IDs and supplementary
groups for both directory search and the target's permissions, but for
.Fn faccessat2
with
.Dv AT_EACCESS ,
below.
The owner bits apply when the real user ID matches the owner;
otherwise the group bits apply when a group matches, and the other bits
apply when none does.
A caller whose real user ID is zero in its user namespace uses its
permitted capabilities for this check.
.Dv CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH
grants read access and directory search.
.Dv CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE
grants the requested access, except that executing a regular file
requires at least one execute bit.
Both capabilities require the inode's owner and group to be mapped.
For example, an existing
.Pa /proc/PID/stat
grants
.Dv F_OK
and
.Dv R_OK
but denies
.Dv W_OK
to a caller whose real user ID is nonzero.
The checks around the mode check come in Linux's order.
.Dv X_OK
of a regular file reached through a
.Cm noexec
mount gives
.Er EACCES
first.
Then
.Dv W_OK
of a regular file, directory or symbolic link on a read-only file
system gives
.Er EROFS
before the mode is read: one mounted read-only, or turned read-only by
a failed write or a halt, the KRFFS boot root among them.
Then
.Dv W_OK
of an immutable file gives
.Er EPERM ,
and then the mode check.
Last,
.Dv W_OK
through a mount made read-only alone, by
.Dv MS_REMOUNT | MS_BIND | MS_RDONLY
over a writable file system, as bubblewrap binds
.Pa /proc/sys ,
gives
.Er EROFS
only after the mode check has passed, so a mode that denies writing
gives
.Er EACCES
there.
A device, FIFO or socket never gives
.Er EROFS .
.Pa /sys
is mounted read-only though its attributes take writes, and gives none.
A followed
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N
is judged as the file its descriptor is open on, through the mount the
descriptor was opened through, by the mode of a named FIFO or of the
device node a device was opened through;
with
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
the link itself is judged, on the mount of
.Pa /proc ,
by the mode its descriptor's access gives it.
Either way the lookup's checks come first, the search of
.Pa /proc/PID/fd ,
the descriptor open and, to follow the link, the process's ptrace read
check, and they too go by the real IDs unless
.Dv AT_EACCESS
is given, as Linux's
.Fn faccessat
lends the real IDs to the whole lookup.
.Pp
Relative paths are resolved from the current working directory.
.Fn faccessat
instead resolves them from the directory descriptor
.Fa dirfd ,
or from the working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
The descriptor is resolved to a path and does not bypass ancestor
search permissions.
Symbolic links are followed, but by
.Fn faccessat2
with
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW .
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
must reach a directory, through a final symbolic link, or the call
returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
.Pp
.Fn faccessat2
is
.Fn faccessat
with
.Fa flags :
.Dv AT_EACCESS
uses the caller's filesystem IDs and effective capabilities,
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
judges a final symbolic link itself, and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
with an empty or null
.Fa path
judges what
.Fa dirfd
is open on, by the owner, group and mode
.Xr fstat 2
reports, or the working directory for
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
A descriptor open on a
.Pa /proc/PID/fd
directory is judged as Linux's
.Fn proc_fd_permission
judges it, by the owner its last lookup gave it, and a thread of that
process passes whatever the mode, with no new lookup.
Access to a held
.Pa /proc/PID
or
.Pa /proc/PID/task
returns
.Er ESRCH
after the process is reaped, even for
.Dv F_OK .
A write check on a read-only superblock returns
.Er EROFS
first.
.Pp
The kernel calls are 33 for
.Fn access ,
307 for
.Fn faccessat ,
which takes only
.Fa dirfd ,
.Fa path
and
.Fa mode ,
and 439 for
.Fn faccessat2 ;
on x86-64 they are 21, 269 and 439.
musl's
.Fn faccessat
uses call 307 for
.Fa flags
of zero and call 439 for any other.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 when all requested checks succeed.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
calls
.Fn faccessat2
with each of its flags.
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/permprobe.c
checks that an unprivileged caller can read
.Pa /hello.txt
but receives
.Er EACCES
when testing it with
.Dv W_OK .
.Pa tests/searchprobe.c
checks real-ID permission decisions with unequal real and effective IDs.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENAMETOOLONG
.It Er EACCES
Search permission is denied on a path component, the target denies a
requested permission, or
.Dv X_OK
asks for a regular file through a
.Cm noexec
mount.
.It Er EBADF
A relative path, or an empty one with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
uses an invalid or stale
.Fa dirfd .
.It Er EBUSY
Path resolution enters a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Er EFAULT
The path cannot be read from user memory.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa mode
has bits other than
.Dv R_OK ,
.Dv W_OK
and
.Dv X_OK ,
or
.Fa flags
has bits other than
.Dv AT_EACCESS ,
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH .
.It Er EIO
Path lookup or metadata access fails, including access to a removed device.
.It Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
The supplied path or its joined absolute form exceeds 4095 bytes, a
directory descriptor's path cannot be reconstructed within its buffer,
a component exceeds 255 bytes, or symbolic-link expansion exceeds
4095 bytes.
.It Er ENOENT
The path is empty without
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
a component is missing, or a symbolic link has an empty target.
.It Er ENOMEM
Path lookup cannot allocate a file-system node.
.It Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory, a relative
path uses a nondirectory
.Fa dirfd ,
or a path that ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
reaches something else.
.It Er EPERM
.Dv W_OK
was requested of an immutable file.
.It Er EROFS
.Dv W_OK
was requested of a regular file, directory or symbolic link on a
read-only file system, or through a mount made read-only alone after
its mode check succeeded.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2
